    "category": "approval",
    "question": "Who is allowed to approve refunds over 50 dollars, and is one approver enough or do larger amounts need a second person?",
    "proposed": "Any member of the support-lead group can approve, and one approver is enough for every amount over 50 dollars."
  },
  {
    "category": "effects",
    "question": "How is the refund actually paid, and what stops the same request from being paid twice?",
    "proposed": "Pay through the existing payment processor's refund API to the original payment method, using the request ID as an idempotency key, with at most one refund per order unless a person overrides."
  },
  {
    "category": "data sensitivity",
    "question": "Can the order text contain personal or payment details that must be removed before it is sent to a model or written to logs?",
    "proposed": "Yes. Redact card numbers, emails, phone numbers and addresses before any model call, and log only the order ID, amounts and decisions."
  },
  {
    "category": "schedule and triggers",
    "question": "What starts the workflow, and should each request be processed immediately or in batches?",
    "proposed": "Start on each new refund request from the support form or ticket webhook and process it immediately, one request at a time."
  },
  {
    "category": "human fallback",
    "question": "What happens when the order text can't be verified or is ambiguous, or when an approver rejects or doesn't respond?",
    "proposed": "Send unverifiable requests to a human review queue. A rejection closes the request and notifies the customer. With no approval response after 48 hours, escalate to the support lead and never pay automatically."
  }
]

